<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Appalachian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Appalachians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Appalachian Mountains are an ancient mountain system of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, stretching about 2,400 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. For British colonists they formed a barrier to westward settlement, marked by the Proclamation Line of 1763, until settlers crossed into Kentucky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a> through passes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> Gap. The southern Appalachians were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, and the region later became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains.jpg" alt="Appalachian Mountains" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J Guth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue_Ridge_Parkway_Sunset_(43691100).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
